{"page_content": "Is the substance classified as acutely toxic by the dermal route (Category 1)?\u00b0 \u2014 Has the substance proven to be a corrosive, irritant or non-irritant in a suitable acute dermal toxicity test? d Has the substance proven to be a corrosive or an irritant in sensitisation studies or after repeated exposure? \u00a9 \u2014 Existing/new (Q)SAR data and read-across Are there structurally related substances (suitable \u201cread-across\u201d or grouping), which are classified as corrosive to the skin (Skin Corrosive Cat. 1), or do suitable (Q)SAR methods indicate corrosion potential of the substance? f _, Are there structurally related substances (suitable classification. Existing data from general toxicity studies via the dermal route and from sensitisation studies YES: The substance will be classified for acute dermal toxicity (column 2 adaptation in section 8.1 of Annexes VII and VIII). No new testing for skin irritation/corrosion is needed in this case. YES: If test conditions are consistent with OECD TG 404, consider classifying accordingly (Skin Corrosive Cat. 1, 1A, 1B, 1C or Skin Irritant Cat. 2) or consider no classification. YES: This information cannot be used for considering a concrete classification conclusion but must be used exclusively within the integrated Weight-of-Evidence judgement. YES: Consider classifying as Skin Corrosive Cat. 1. YES:", "metadata": {"doc_id": "c50503fa-ec90-4ee3-9b33-7f745adbb2f8", "content_type": "Table", "filename": "information_requirements_r7a_en_part_2.pdf"}}